Terminator movies some are good some are  bad and some are harder to watch than a  nuclear apocalypse here's what we love  and hate about this iconic film  franchise as maligned as its reputation  may be among fans and critics Terminator  Salvation is a criminally  underappreciated entry in The Wider  Terminator franchise one of the things  that makes the 2009 film stand apart  from the rest is that it makes an effort  to break from the usual narrative  formula of the series trying to change  what a Terminator movie could be though  the film itself leaves room for  improvement salvation offers a  surprisingly refreshing installment in  the franchise especially compared to its  more contrived follow-ups let me if you  want to live after several movies  teasing an epic war for survival between  humanity and sentient machines  Terminator Salvation is actually set  during the post-apocalyptic conflict  after Skynet has Unleashed a devastating  nuclear attack rather than relying on  the standard Terminator plotline of  protagonists on the run from a  Relentless killing machine salvation is  a war Story one that finds Humanity on  the back foot instead of a prophesized  hero embracing their wartime Destiny  salvation follows a rugged Survivor as  he attempts to navigate the war-torn  world and it's all the better for it  the most obvious shortcoming of the  Terminator franchise is that there are  way too many sequels and reboots to the  first two movies these follow-ups tend  to get lost in their own failed attempts  to continue the original story often  playing with continuity in radically  different ways while noticeably  suffering from diminishing returns and  quality since 1991's Judgment Day there  have been four additional Terminator  movies none of which reached the  critical Acclaim of the first two films  the franchise reached its low point in  2015 with Terminator Genesis which holds  a paltry 26 percent critic score on  Rotten Tomatoes though 2019's Terminator  dark fate enjoyed the highest Acclaim  since Judgment Day with a 70 percent  Rotten Tomato score it was still a  financial disappointment earning just  261 million dollars at the worldwide box  office  composer Brad Fidel's theme and score  for the first two Terminator movies are  relatively simple but instantly  recognizable the 1984 original film  features a stripped-down industrial  sound driven largely by synthesizers  oscillators and ambient noise to set the  mood these minimalist sensibilities  balance the grounded 80s setting with  the futuristic Stakes at play Fidel  returned to score Judgment Day for the  sequel he maintained the iconic themes  from his previous work while upping the  ante in terms of instrumentation  specifically he introduced full  orchestration to the score allowing for  a Fuller and warmer sound in order to  take advantage of the heightened scope  and Elevate the story's quieter moments  Fidel's work on the score for the first  two movies subtly laid the musical  foundations for the entire franchise  Anna's influence has been felt across  every subsequent film  what day is it the day 12 May Thursday  what year the first few Terminator  movies take a relatively simple approach  to time travel mechanics soldiers from  the Future travel back in time to alter  the course of history by killing those  who would shape Humanity's Destiny  salvation largely shirks the time travel  component of the franchise altogether  telling a complete story set within one  relative period without anyone traveling  through the time stream this tight  approach to time travel descends into  progressively more complicated timeline  mechanics after salvation however making  these science fiction elements more  confusing and intrusive to each film's  story Genesis introduces the concepts of  temporal paradoxes and Divergent  timelines caused by tampering with the  space-time Continuum with the characters  lives affected by these drastic changes  dark fate goes one step further by  completely changing the future conflict  as a different sentient AI replaces  Skynet and successfully assassin nates  John Connor before he can save Humanity  disrupting the original timeline both of  these films take a relatively clean take  on the concept of time travel and model  it leading to a confusing and entirely  unnecessary mishmash of continuities  the first couple of Terminator movies  use pacing in a similar way with the  Terminators scouring the Greater Los  Angeles area for their intended targets  the lead characters are forced on the  run before regrouping to take on the  antagonist this premise provides a  perfect opportunity for filmmakers to  ratchet up the tension as the heroes lie  low only barely evading their cybernetic  pursuers before the big action set  pieces kick off when the movies do cut  loose for these action sequences they  deliver on this sudden cathartic release  of tension and suspense to great effect  all in all the Terminator movies are a  master class and action movie pacing  knowing just when to come up for air  before diving right back in the  Terminator franchise has always enjoyed  the occasional moment of humor employed  throughout the movies to break up  detention for the audience with the  film's following Judgment Day tend to  forget however is that the funnier  moments in the first two movies were  subtly employed so as to not detract  from The Narrative stakes and  overarching tone the questionable use of  humor is apparent as early as Terminator  3 rise of the Machines and does not  noticeably improve in its follow-ups  talk to the hand Genesis and dark fate  continue to poke fun at the characters  in increasingly awkward ways with the  latter portraying a retired T800 who  became an interior decorator though the  early movies were not without levity the  later Terminator entries sense of humor  Ventures into camp and self-parody in  ways that threaten to derail each  Storey's atmosphere  while the franchise as a whole is known  for its bombastic action the original  Terminator opts for a more grounded  intimate approach to its Thrills the  first movie is almost paced like a  slasher movie as the T800 brutally  eliminates anyone who gets in its way  the only major things differentiating  the Terminator from typical slashers is  a preference for Guns Over Knives and a  metallic endoskeleton of course these  horrific sensibilities are on full  display when the T800 breaks into  Sarah's apartment and kills her roommate  Ginger along with Ginger's boyfriend  Sarah desperately calls her apartment to  warn her friends of the threat but  they're already preoccupied in true  slasher movie fashion after their  romantic interlude the couple are  murdered by their stalker this moment  showcases just how Relentless the  Terminator can be while demonstrating  that the movie is just as terrifying as  it is exciting  I'll be back Arnold Schwarzenegger's  career was catapulted to new heights by  the Terminator and the franchise has  always maintained its connection to the  legendary Action Hero though  Schwarzenegger is a welcome sight in the  first few films the franchise goes to  ridiculous lengths to bring him back and  allow him to reprise his star-making  role by salvation it starts to feel like  the narrative needs to move Beyond  Schwarzenegger but the film series  itself has always existed in the actor's  shadow in a way that prevents it from  forging new ground salvation was  produced while Schwarzenegger was  serving as governor of California  prompting the 2009 movie to use CGI and  a body double instead the appearance  feels forced and distracts from the  movie's climax rather than adding any  sense of resonance or relevance to the  finale dark fate also shoehorns  Schwarzenegger's t-800 into the story  with the cyborg living in self-imposed  retirement for decades before emerging  to conveniently team up with Sarah late  in the film it's past time for the  tournament Nader franchise to move past  its biggest star  Judgment Day introduces the Fearsome  T-1000 a more advanced Terminator from  the post-apocalyptic future the t-1000's  unique composition allows him to  transform his body into different forms  as he stalks John and Sarah Connor a  breathtaking technical achievement in  1991 the special effects to bring the  T-1000 to life still hold up today as a  visually impressive feat James Cameron  worked closely with industrial Light and  Magic on Judgment Day with approximately  5 million dollars from the production  budget spent on visual effects this  investment more than paid off with the  liquid metal arguably standing out as  the most memorable visual effect in the  franchise  the Terminator films following Judgment  Day each have a tendency to employ  twists late in the story to surprise  their audiences and up the stakes  however these twists rarely connect with  audiences and serve more as distractions  than anything else for example  Terminator Genesis posits that an  alternate timeline version of John has  been transformed into a time-traveling  Terminator himself dark fate meanwhile  reveals that the retired T800 has  secretly been feeding Sarah information  to track down other Terminators a plot  point that makes less and less sense the  more one thinks about it still it could  always be worse in the unused original  ending for Terminator Salvation John  Connor is killed off altogether before  his face is grafted onto Marcus's who  then unceremoniously massacres the rest  of the movie's lead characters  at a time when female action  protagonists were a rare side in major  Hollywood Productions Linda Hamilton's  Sarah Connor became one of the most  recognizable Sci-Fi Action heroes in  history the Terminator sees Sarah  transform from a normal woman in a  dead-end job to a hardened fighter who  delivers the killing blow against the  t-800 this character Evolution continued  into Judgment Day and dark fate with  Sarah becoming a seasoned veteran who is  quite capable of taking down Terminators  by herself I'll be back  [Music]  Hamilton's performance is a huge part of  Sarah Connor's depiction not just as a  Survivor but as a warrior too Hamilton  made Sarah a Bonafide Action Hero  channeling her trauma into a Steely  resolve to balance this Hamilton  displays a tragic vulnerability in Sarah  too giving the franchise its most  nuanced performance by far  there are a few effective plot twists in  The Terminator franchise but some of  them were infamously spoiled for  audiences by the movie's marketing  campaigns in the beginning of Judgment  Day the t-800 is positioned as another  model sent back to assassinate John  before it saves him from the T-1000 in  the nick of time it's a thrilling twist  if you don't know it's coming but this  heroic change in direction for the T800  was spoiled in the film's trailers this  time there are two Terminator two  meanwhile salvation revolves around  Marcus discovering he's a cyborg after  he's inspected by human Freedom Fighters  during the war against the machines this  twist was similarly spoiled in the  trailers and television ads for the film  as a result audiences were aware of  Marcus's cybernetic secret long before  he discovered it for himself  armed with a whole arsenal of weaponry  and some of the most iconic catchphrases  in cinematic history Arnold  Schwarzenegger became a household name  thanks to the Terminator the 1984 film  almost totally revolves around  Schwarzenegger's performance as the  t-800 with his stoic demeanor and  chiseled physique defining the franchise  as a whole seven years after portraying  the ultimate sci-fi antagonist  Schwarzenegger's turn is a very  different T800 in Judgment Day  effectively reframed him as the heroic  face of the franchise there's a reason  why the film series keeps bringing back  Schwarzenegger to reprise his fan  favorite role even when it works to the  story's detriment decades after his  debut as the t-800 Schwarzenegger never  feels like he's phoning it in as the  futuristic killer even with lesser  material to work with in later films  the Terminator series is prone to the  worst kind of sequels ones that not only  dilute the quality of the franchise but  also actively make plot points from  previous installments Irrelevant this  unfortunate Trend begins as early as  Judgment Day with the Revelation that  Terminators have shifted their  priorities towards assassinating Jon  after failing to kill Sarah however the  subsequent set of sequels do even  further harm to the overarching story  and its Legacy for example rise of the  Machines reveals that machine-driven  Armageddon was inevitable all along  rendering Sarah and John's efforts in  the second movie totally useless dark  fate is the most egregious of all in  this regard with the movie opening with  a T800 successfully assassinating Jon  before he can fulfill his Destiny this  nullifies the struggles that Sarah  endured to survive in the preceding  films and the sequel scrambles to find  new ways to continue the story  [Music]
 

